  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain-free conditions will persist through the end \nof October and could even extend into the first few days of \nNovember \n\nA large area of high pressure will remain in place over the\nNortheast U.S. and Central Appalachians, maintaining mainly clear\nskies and rain-free conditions right through the upcoming week. \n\nModerate variations will occur in daily and nocturnal\ntemperatures as at least one weak, dry cold fronts move SE \nacross the Commonwealth during the upcoming 5-6 days. Wind \ndirection/speed and cloud cover will also show some minor day- \nday variations. \n\nThe first of the aforementioned dry cold fronts will slip SE\nacross the CWA Monday night into early Tuesday, but with only \nlimited moisture to work with, just some periods of BKN, shallow\nstrato cu and cirrus will occur with no rainfall expected beyond\na few sprinkles over the Northern Mtns Monday night. \n\nAfter a brief shot of seasonably cool air Tuesday and Wed \n(directed mainly at the NE half of the state), a late \nweek/Saturday rebound in temps (to well above normal values in \nthe low-mid 60s across the Mtns and upper 60s to mid 70s in the \nCentral and Southern Valleys) will follow. A second, weak\nfrontal boundary could brush by across the Northern tier\ncounties Friday afternoon and night.",
